Live endpoint evidence: POST /api/canvas-sync discovered, not probed (state-changing); GET /api/courses returned 200 in 43ms; GET /api/courses/{id:guid} discovered, not probed (needs a real id and none was available from a sibling list endpoint); GET /api/tasks returned 200 in 39ms; GET /api/tasks/{id:guid} discovered, not probed (needs a real id and none was available from a sibling list endpoint); POST /api/tasks discovered, not probed (state-changing); POST /api/tasks/ai-description discovered, not probed (state-changing); POST /api/tasks/{id:guid}/ai-breakdown discovered, not probed (state-changing); PUT /api/tasks/{id:guid} discovered, not probed (state-changing); DELETE /api/tasks/{id:guid} discovered, not probed (state-changing).

IMPLEMENTATION: Route surface itself is fine, but flagging an operational gap: `student-3-backend` has two hard runtime dependencies over HTTP (`ISharedCanvasClient` -> `shared-backend`, `IAiTaskService` -> `ai-mode`), and neither `AddHttpClient<ISharedCanvasClient, SharedCanvasClient>()` nor `AddHttpClient<IAiTaskService, AiTaskService>()` in `Program.cs` is configured with a timeout or retry policy - a slow/unresponsive `shared-backend` or `ai-mode` could hang a request indefinitely. There is also no `/health/live` or `/health/ready` endpoint registered anywhere in `Program.cs`, so Docker Compose (or anything else) has no way to detect that this service is degraded because one of its two dependencies is down, versus genuinely healthy. Recommend adding a resilience handler (retry + timeout) on both `HttpClient` registrations and a couple of `IHealthCheck`s so orchestration can distinguish "backend is up" from "backend can actually serve requests".

REVIEW: Agree - this is evidence-backed (confirmed absent from `Program.cs` and the discovered route set has no health endpoints at all) rather than a speculative best-practice nag, and it's a real availability risk given this service already depends on two other HTTP services per the architecture docs. Worth prioritizing given the PR is explicitly framed as "general fixups".

